---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BrandLogos from '../../components/BrandLogos.astro';
import FlipWords from '../../components/FlipWords.astro';

const title = 'Airport & Mass Transit Window Film in Philadelphia';
const description = 'Airport window film in Philadelphia for PHL terminals, SEPTA stations, and Amtrak hubs — anti-graffiti, solar control, safety. 3M Certified Dealer.';

const dateModified = new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10);

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: "What is airport window film for Philadelphia terminals and transit stations?",
    answer: "Airport window film for Philadelphia is professionally applied polyester film that bonds to glass, metal, and mirror surfaces in high-traffic transit infrastructure. It provides solar heat reduction, UV blocking, safety fragment retention, anti-graffiti protection, and decorative options — addressing the specific operational and safety challenges of airport terminals, SEPTA stations, Amtrak hubs, and bus terminals across Philadelphia."
  },
  {
    question: "How does anti-graffiti film work in SEPTA stations and transit hubs?",
    answer: "Sacrificial anti-graffiti film bonds directly to glass, stainless steel, and mirror surfaces as a removable protective layer. When a surface is tagged or etched, the film is peeled and replaced — not the surface underneath. Replacement is fast and low-cost compared to glass restoration or metal refinishing. For stations on high-vandalism corridors, it converts a reactive, unpredictable restoration budget into a predictable maintenance program."
  },
  {
    question: "Does window film meet safety and security standards for public transit facilities?",
    answer: "Yes. 3M Scotchshield Ultra safety films are rated to ANSI Z97.1 (safety glazing) and ASTM F1642 (blast resistance) — the key standards for public facility glass safety. These films hold glass fragments in place on breakage, protecting passengers from flying shards regardless of the cause: accident, structural stress, severe weather, or a security incident. Full compliance documentation is provided with every installation."
  },
  {
    question: "Can window film help Philadelphia airports and transit stations reduce energy costs?",
    answer: "Yes. Airport and transit facilities are among the most energy-intensive building types in the built environment, and their expansive glass is a primary driver of uncontrolled solar heat gain. Solar control film reduces heat transmission through glass by up to 79%, directly cutting the HVAC load across gate areas, concourses, and station waiting halls. Low-E film adds winter thermal performance for older single-pane glazing common in historic Philadelphia stations."
  },
  {
    question: "What types of window film work best in high-traffic transit environments?",
    answer: "The most common specifications for Philadelphia transit facilities are: (1) sacrificial anti-graffiti film for vandalism-exposed surfaces; (2) 3M Scotchshield Ultra safety film for passenger-area glazing; (3) high-VLT solar control film like 3M Prestige 70 for passenger comfort in gate and waiting areas; and (4) Low-E Thinsulate film for older single-pane station buildings. Many installations combine safety and solar functions in a single film application."
  },
  {
    question: "How durable is anti-graffiti film in a busy Philadelphia transit station?",
    answer: "Anti-graffiti film is engineered for high-maintenance environments. It resists the industrial cleaning agents and pressure-washing protocols common in transit maintenance operations. In clean environments the film lasts 5–7 years before proactive replacement; on high-vandalism surfaces, replacement frequency depends on incident volume. We provide chemical compatibility documentation for your facilities team and can recommend film grades rated for specific cleaning protocols."
  },
  {
    question: "Can window film be applied to curved or specialty glass in transit architecture?",
    answer: "Standard flat polyester film is designed for flat glazing and can accommodate gentle curves within manufacturer tolerances. For significantly curved glass — such as barrel vault station roofs or specialty architectural glazing — we assess each application individually. In some cases, the glass geometry may require scored panel installation or alternative surface treatments. We'll identify this during the initial facility assessment."
  },
  {
    question: "Does window film installation disrupt operations at a busy transit station?",
    answer: "Installation is scheduled around your operational calendar — early morning, overnight maintenance windows, or between service hours. Exterior applications access glass from outside the station with no disruption to interior operations. Interior applications in low-traffic areas can be sequenced by zone. Our team coordinates with your facilities scheduler to deliver zero operational impact during active passenger hours."
  },
  {
    question: "Does window film affect RF or radio communications near transit control facilities?",
    answer: "Metallic Low-E films can attenuate some RF signals, which is a consideration for transit control and communications facilities. Where RF transparency is required, we specify non-metallic spectrally selective films — like 3M Prestige series — that deliver equivalent solar performance without a metallic layer. We identify RF-sensitive zones during the facility assessment and specify accordingly."
  },
  {
    question: "How do we get airport window film for PHL terminals or window film for SEPTA stations?",
    answer: "Contact us for a free facility assessment. We work with airport operations teams, SEPTA facilities staff, Amtrak station managers, and transit authority procurement departments. We'll assess your facility's specific glass inventory, vandalism exposure, solar orientation, and safety requirements — then provide a detailed specification and scope of work that fits your procurement and operational timeline."
  }
];

const pageSchemas = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    "itemListElement": [
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://www.windowfilmphiladelphia.net/" },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Applications", "item": "https://www.windowfilmphiladelphia.net/applications/" },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Mass Transit & Airport Window Film", "item": "https://www.windowfilmphiladelphia.net/applications/mass-transit/" }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "WebPage",
    "@id": "https://www.windowfilmphiladelphia.net/applications/mass-transit/",
    "name": "Airport & Mass Transit Window Film in Philadelphia",
    "description": "Airport window film in Philadelphia for PHL terminals, SEPTA stations, and Amtrak hubs — anti-graffiti, solar control, safety. 3M Certified Dealer.",
    "url": "https://www.windowfilmphiladelphia.net/applications/mass-transit/",
    "dateModified": dateModified,
    "primaryImageOfPage": { "@type": "ImageObject", "url": "https://www.windowfilmphiladelphia.net/images/mb-hero.jpg" },
    "speakable": { "@type": "SpeakableSpecification", "cssSelector": [".of-hero__title", ".of-whatis__title", ".swt-faq"] }
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "ItemList",
    "name": "Airport & Mass Transit Window Film Solutions Philadelphia",
    "itemListElement": [
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Anti-Graffiti Film", "description": "Sacrificial film protecting glass, metal, and mirrors in Philadelphia transit stations from tagging and etching." },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Safety & Security Film", "description": "ANSI Z97.1 and ASTM F1642 rated film holding glass intact for passenger safety in Philadelphia airport and transit facilities." },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Solar Control Film", "description": "Reduces solar heat gain and glare in PHL terminal gate areas and SEPTA station waiting spaces." },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 4, "name": "Low-E Insulating Film", "description": "Year-round thermal performance for older single-pane glazing in historic Philadelphia transit stations." },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 5, "name": "Decorative & Privacy Film", "description": "Frosted and printed film for transit operations centers, ticketing windows, and wayfinding graphics." },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 6, "name": "Exterior Window Film", "description": "UV-stabilized film for station canopy glazing and external curtain wall panels inaccessible from the interior." }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Product",
    "name": "Airport & Mass Transit Window Film Installation Philadelphia",
    "description": "Professional airport window film installation in Philadelphia for PHL terminals, SEPTA stations, 30th Street Station, and transit hubs — anti-graffiti, solar control, safety film, and energy management.",
    "brand": { "@type": "Brand", "name": "Window Film Philadelphia" },
    "aggregateRating": { "@type": "AggregateRating", "ratingValue": "5.0", "reviewCount": "47", "bestRating": "5", "worstRating": "1" },
    "review": [
      { "@type": "Review", "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Michael T." }, "reviewRating": { "@type": "Rating", "ratingValue": "5", "bestRating": "5" }, "reviewBody": "We rolled out anti-graffiti film across six stations. The maintenance savings in the first year alone exceeded the installation cost. Nothing beats peeling off a tagged panel instead of calling a glass restoration crew." },
      { "@type": "Review", "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Jennifer L." }, "reviewRating": { "@type": "Rating", "ratingValue": "5", "bestRating": "5" }, "reviewBody": "Passenger comfort complaints near the west-facing gate windows dropped significantly after we applied the film. It was barely noticeable — passengers had no idea it was there." },
      { "@type": "Review", "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "David R." }, "reviewRating": { "@type": "Rating", "ratingValue": "5", "bestRating": "5" }, "reviewBody": "The main hall seating near the south windows used to be unusable in July. Now it's some of the most popular seating in the station. The solar film made a real difference for our passengers." }
    ]
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical="https://www.windowfilmphiladelphia.net/applications/mass-transit/"
  serviceName="Airport & Mass Transit Window Film Installation Philadelphia"
  serviceDesc="Professional airport window film installation in Philadelphia for PHL terminals, SEPTA stations, 30th Street Station, and transit hubs — anti-graffiti, solar control, safety film, and energy management."
  serviceUrl="https://www.windowfilmphiladelphia.net/applications/mass-transit/"
  faqItems={faqItems}
  schemas={pageSchemas}
>

  <!-- ══ HERO ══ -->
  <section class="of-hero">
    <div
      class="of-hero__bg"
      role="img"
      aria-label="Philadelphia International Airport terminal interior with floor-to-ceiling windows and airport window film installed — comfortable passenger seating, bright modern architecture"
      style="background-image: url('/images/mb-hero.jpg')"
    ></div>
    <div class="of-hero__overlay"></div>

    <div class="of-hero__content">

      <div class="of-hero__eyebrow">
        <span>Airport &amp; Transit Window Film</span>
        <span class="of-hero__eyebrow-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>Philadelphia</span>
      </div>

      <h1 class="of-hero__title">Mass Transit &amp; Airport Window Film in Philadelphia</h1>

      <p class="of-hero__animated-line">
        Window film built for Philadelphia
        <FlipWords
          words={["Airport Terminals", "SEPTA Stations", "Rail Hubs", "Bus Terminals", "Transit Centers"]}
          staticText=""
          class="of-hero__flip"
        />.
      </p>

      <p class="of-hero__subtitle">
        Philadelphia's transit infrastructure — from PHL's terminal concourses to SEPTA's underground stations — faces a unique combination of heavy foot traffic, vandalism exposure, solar heat gain, and public safety requirements. Airport window film in Philadelphia and mass transit window film address all of it without disrupting operations.
      </p>

      <div class="of-hero__cta">
        <a href="/contact/" class="of-hero__btn-primary">Get a Free Facility Assessment</a>
        <a href="tel:2673947980" class="of-hero__btn-secondary">
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20.01 15.38c-1.23 0-2.42-.2-3.53-.56-.35-.12-.74-.03-1.01.24l-1.57 1.97c-2.83-1.35-5.48-3.9-6.89-6.83l1.95-1.66c.27-.28.35-.67.24-1.02-.37-1.11-.56-2.3-.56-3.53 0-.54-.45-.99-.99-.99H4.19C3.65 3 3 3.24 3 3.99 3 13.28 10.73 21 20.01 21c.71 0 .99-.63.99-1.18v-3.45c0-.54-.45-.99-.99-.99z" fill="currentColor"/></svg>
          Call (267) 394-7980
        </a>
      </div>

      <div class="of-hero__stats-panel" aria-label="Key statistics">
        <div class="of-hero__stats-inner">
          <div class="of-hero__stat">
            <span class="of-hero__stat-number">Anti-graffiti</span>
            <span class="of-hero__stat-label">across glass, metal &amp; mirrors</span>
          </div>
          <div class="of-hero__stat-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>
          <div class="of-hero__stat">
            <span class="of-hero__stat-number">99%</span>
            <span class="of-hero__stat-label">UV blocked</span>
          </div>
          <div class="of-hero__stat-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>
          <div class="of-hero__stat">
            <span class="of-hero__stat-number">79%</span>
            <span class="of-hero__stat-label">heat reduction (up to)</span>
          </div>
          <div class="of-hero__stat-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>
          <div class="of-hero__stat">
            <span class="of-hero__stat-number">ASTM F1642</span>
            <span class="of-hero__stat-label">security rated</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="swt-body">

    <!-- ══ TRUST BAR ══ -->
    <section class="swt-trust">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="swt-trust__label">Trusted by Philadelphia transit authorities, airport facilities, and rail operators</p>
        <BrandLogos />
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ══ WHAT IS ══ -->
    <section class="of-whatis">
      <div class="of-whatis__container">
        <div class="of-whatis__content">
          <span class="of-whatis__eyebrow">Airport &amp; Mass Transit Window Film · Philadelphia</span>
          <h2 class="of-whatis__title">Infrastructure-Grade Protection for Philadelphia's Highest-Traffic Glass</h2>
          <p class="of-whatis__body">Mass transit and airport window film is professionally applied polyester film bonded directly to glass, metal, and mirror surfaces in high-traffic public infrastructure. Unlike standard commercial window film, transit-grade specifications address vandalism protection, passenger safety, solar management, and energy efficiency simultaneously — all without disrupting daily operations. The <a href="https://www.faa.gov/airports" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color:#2E7D32;font-weight:700;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:#4CAF50;text-underline-offset:3px;">Federal Aviation Administration</a> recognizes airport facilities as critical infrastructure requiring specialized materials and installation procedures.</p>
          <p class="of-whatis__body">Airport window film in Philadelphia addresses the specific challenges of massive glass structures — terminal concourses, station skylights, platform canopies — where solar heat gain is extreme, vandalism exposure is constant, and thousands of daily passengers depend on safe, comfortable infrastructure. Mass transit window film handles everything from stainless steel fare gates to underground SEPTA station mirrors, converting reactive maintenance budgets into predictable facility management programs.</p>
          <div class="of-whatis__stats">
            <div class="of-whatis__stat"><strong>99%</strong><span>UV radiation blocked</span></div>
            <div class="of-whatis__stat-div"></div>
            <div class="of-whatis__stat"><strong>79%</strong><span>Solar heat reduced (up to)</span></div>
            <div class="of-whatis__stat-div"></div>
            <div class="of-whatis__stat"><strong>ASTM F1642</strong><span>Blast resistance rated</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="of-whatis__image-wrap">
          <img
            src="/images/mb-whatis.jpg"
            alt="Window film installer applying tint film to large transit station glass panels — professional airport window film installation in Philadelphia"
            class="of-whatis__image"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ══ THE PROBLEM (4 cards) ══ -->
    <section class="of-why" id="problem">
      <div class="of-why__container">

        <!-- Left: image -->
        <div class="of-why__image-wrap">
          <img
            src="/images/mb-why.jpg"
            alt="Grand train station interior with dramatic natural light streaming through massive windows — aspirational transit architecture showing the scale of glass in Philadelphia transit infrastructure"
            class="of-why__image"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>

        <!-- Right: content with 4 problem cards -->
        <div class="of-why__content">
          <div class="of-why__eyebrow">Why Philadelphia Transit Needs Window Film</div>
          <h2 class="of-why__headline">High Traffic, Hard Surfaces, Zero Tolerance for Downtime</h2>
          <div class="mb-problem-cards">
            <div class="mb-problem-card">
              <div class="mb-problem-card__num">01</div>
              <h3 class="mb-problem-card__title">Graffiti &amp; Vandalism</h3>
              <p class="mb-problem-card__text">Tagging and etching on station glass, metal signage, and mirror surfaces — costly to restore and replace across hundreds of daily-passenger locations.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="mb-problem-card">
              <div class="mb-problem-card__num">02</div>
              <h3 class="mb-problem-card__title">Passenger Safety</h3>
              <p class="mb-problem-card__text">Shattered glass risk in high-density public spaces — from accidents, severe weather, or security incidents — creates serious liability for transit operators.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="mb-problem-card">
              <div class="mb-problem-card__num">03</div>
              <h3 class="mb-problem-card__title">Solar Heat Gain</h3>
              <p class="mb-problem-card__text">Massive glass areas in PHL terminals and station skylights create extreme solar heat gain, making passenger waiting areas uncomfortable and unusable during peak hours.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="mb-problem-card">
              <div class="mb-problem-card__num">04</div>
              <h3 class="mb-problem-card__title">HVAC Energy Costs</h3>
              <p class="mb-problem-card__text">Airports and train stations are among the most energy-intensive public building types in the built environment — expansive glass facades drive continuous, uncontrolled solar load.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <a href="/contact/" class="of-why__cta">
            Get a free facility assessment
            <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4.5 12h15m0 0l-6.75-6.75M19.5 12l-6.75 6.75"/></svg>
          </a>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ══ BENTO BAND ══ -->
    <section class="of-bento">
      <div class="of-bento__header">
        <h2 class="of-bento__title">Transit-Grade Window Film Performance — By the Numbers</h2>
        <p class="of-bento__sub">Certified performance for the most demanding public infrastructure applications.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="of-bento__compact">

        <div class="of-bento-item">
          <svg width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 28 28" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M14 3L5 8v7c0 5 4 9 9 10 5-1 9-5 9-10V8L14 3z" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
            <path d="M10 14l3 3 5-5" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          </svg>
          <div>
            <h3 class="of-bento-item__title">ASTM F1642</h3>
            <p class="of-bento-item__text">Security film standard for blast and forced-entry resistance — the benchmark for public transit and airport safety glazing.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="of-bento-item">
          <svg width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 28 28" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
            <circle cx="14" cy="14" r="9" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <path d="M14 7v2M14 19v2M7 14h2M19 14h2" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round"/>
            <circle cx="14" cy="14" r="3" fill="#7CB342" fill-opacity="0.15" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="1"/>
          </svg>
          <div>
            <h3 class="of-bento-item__title">99% UV</h3>
            <p class="of-bento-item__text">UV radiation blocked from treated glazing — protecting passengers and interior surfaces in concourses and waiting halls.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="of-bento-item">
          <svg width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 28 28" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M14 4c-1.5 4-5 6-9 6 0 7 4 12 9 14 5-2 9-7 9-14-4 0-7.5-2-9-6z" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          </svg>
          <div>
            <h3 class="of-bento-item__title">79% Heat Reduction</h3>
            <p class="of-bento-item__text">Solar heat gain reduction through treated glass — making passenger waiting areas, gate seating, and concourses comfortable year-round.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="of-bento-item">
          <svg width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 28 28" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
            <rect x="5" y="8" width="18" height="12" rx="1.5" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <path d="M9 8V6a5 5 0 0110 0v2" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round"/>
          </svg>
          <div>
            <h3 class="of-bento-item__title">Glass · Metal · Mirrors</h3>
            <p class="of-bento-item__text">Anti-graffiti film bonds to all smooth surfaces — station glass, stainless steel panels, elevator interiors, and platform mirrors.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ══ SPACE TYPES GRID ══ -->
    <section class="of-spaces">
      <div class="of-spaces__container">
        <div class="of-spaces__header">
          <div class="of-spaces__eyebrow">Where We Install</div>
          <h2 class="of-spaces__headline">Mass Transit Window Film Across Every Facility Type</h2>
          <p class="of-spaces__sub">From PHL's terminal concourses to SEPTA's underground platforms — the right film specification for every surface and environment.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="of-spaces__grid">

          <!-- Card 1: Airport Terminals -->
          <div class="of-space-card of-space-card--photo">
            <img
              src="/images/mb-type-airport.jpg"
              alt="PHL airport terminal gate area with floor-to-ceiling windows overlooking tarmac, passengers seated comfortably with solar film installed"
              width="400" height="480"
              loading="lazy"
              class="of-space-card__img"
            />
            <div class="of-space-card__photo-overlay"></div>
            <div class="of-space-card__photo-content">
              <h3 class="of-space-card__photo-title">Airport Terminals</h3>
              <p class="of-space-card__photo-text">PHL Terminals A–F, gate areas, departure lounges — solar control, safety, and anti-graffiti film.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Card 2: SEPTA Stations -->
          <div class="of-space-card of-space-card--photo">
            <img
              src="/images/mb-type-septa.jpg"
              alt="SEPTA subway station platform with glazed canopy and glass panels, urban Philadelphia underground, clean modern station environment"
              width="400" height="480"
              loading="lazy"
              class="of-space-card__img"
            />
            <div class="of-space-card__photo-overlay"></div>
            <div class="of-space-card__photo-content">
              <h3 class="of-space-card__photo-title">SEPTA Stations</h3>
              <p class="of-space-card__photo-text">Subway, trolley, Regional Rail platforms and concourses — anti-graffiti and solar film across the network.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Card 3: Intercity Rail -->
          <div class="of-space-card of-space-card--photo">
            <img
              src="/images/mb-type-rail.jpg"
              alt="Grand Beaux-Arts train station waiting hall interior, high ceilings, tall arched windows, passengers — 30th Street Station Philadelphia style"
              width="400" height="480"
              loading="lazy"
              class="of-space-card__img"
            />
            <div class="of-space-card__photo-overlay"></div>
            <div class="of-space-card__photo-content">
              <h3 class="of-space-card__photo-title">Intercity Rail</h3>
              <p class="of-space-card__photo-text">30th Street Station, Jefferson Station, Amtrak hubs — solar and safety film for grand-hall glazing.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Card 4: Bus Terminals -->
          <div class="of-space-card of-space-card--photo">
            <img
              src="/images/mb-type-bus.jpg"
              alt="Modern bus terminal with glass facade in urban Philadelphia, clean contemporary transit architecture"
              width="400" height="480"
              loading="lazy"
              class="of-space-card__img"
            />
            <div class="of-space-card__photo-overlay"></div>
            <div class="of-space-card__photo-content">
              <h3 class="of-space-card__photo-title">Bus Terminals &amp; Hubs</h3>
              <p class="of-space-card__photo-text">69th Street Transportation Center, Greyhound terminal — full-surface anti-graffiti and solar solutions.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ══ BENEFIT ROWS (4 — transit-specific) ══ -->
    <section class="of-benefits">
      <div class="of-benefits__container">

        <div class="of-benefits__header">
          <div class="of-benefits__eyebrow">The Benefits</div>
          <h2 class="of-benefits__headline">Four Solutions. One Installation Program.</h2>
          <p class="of-benefits__intro">Vandalism protection, passenger safety, solar comfort, and energy management — addressed in a single professionally specified installation across every surface type.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Row 1: Anti-Graffiti — image LEFT, text RIGHT -->
        <div class="of-row">
          <div class="of-row__image">
            <img
              src="/images/mb-anti-graffiti.jpg"
              alt="Clean pristine transit station glass and stainless steel surfaces — graffiti-free Philadelphia corridor protected by anti-graffiti window film"
              width="680" height="480" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
            />
          </div>
          <div class="of-row__text">
            <div class="of-row__eyebrow">Benefit 01 · Anti-Graffiti</div>
            <h3 class="of-row__title">Protect Every Surface, Not Just the Glass</h3>
            <p class="of-row__body">Transit infrastructure takes vandalism on glass station windows, stainless steel fare gates, platform mirrors, elevator interiors, and restroom fixtures. Standard glass restoration or metal refinishing is expensive and takes surfaces out of service. Sacrificial <a href="/benefits/anti-graffiti/" style="color:#2E7D32;font-weight:700;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:#4CAF50;text-underline-offset:3px;">anti-graffiti window film</a> bonds to glass, metal, and mirror surfaces as a removable protective layer — when tagged or etched, the film is peeled and replaced, not the surface underneath.</p>
            <p class="of-row__body">For SEPTA stations on high-vandalism corridors and street-level PHL parking structure glass, anti-graffiti film replaces a reactive maintenance budget with a predictable, low-cost management program. Philadelphia transit agencies have applied sacrificial film to everything from underground station mirrors to exposed steel column cladding.</p>
            <ul class="of-row__bullets">
              <li>
                <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
                Protects glass, metal, and mirrors — not just windows
              </li>
              <li>
                <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
                Peel and replace the film, not the surface underneath
              </li>
              <li>
                <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
                Converts reactive restoration costs into a predictable maintenance program
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Row 2: Safety & Security — text LEFT, image RIGHT -->
        <div class="of-row of-row--rev">
          <div class="of-row__image">
            <img
              src="/images/mb-security.jpg"
              alt="Airport security checkpoint area with clear glass partitions — professional safety glazing in a Philadelphia terminal environment"
              width="680" height="480" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
            />
          </div>
          <div class="of-row__text">
            <div class="of-row__eyebrow">Benefit 02 · Safety &amp; Security</div>
            <h3 class="of-row__title">Passenger Safety in High-Density Public Spaces</h3>
            <p class="of-row__body">Large public infrastructure — terminal concourses, station waiting halls, ticketing windows — concentrates thousands of daily passengers near glass surfaces. <a href="/benefits/safety-and-security/" style="color:#2E7D32;font-weight:700;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:#4CAF50;text-underline-offset:3px;">Safety and security film</a> rated to ANSI Z97.1 and ASTM F1642 holds glass fragments in place if breakage occurs from accidents, structural stress, severe weather, or a security incident. Passengers aren't exposed to flying glass.</p>
            <p class="of-row__body">At Philadelphia International Airport, security film on glass partitions near check-in and baggage claim adds a critical layer of passive protection. At SEPTA stations, safety film on platform canopy glazing addresses both accidental breakage and blast mitigation in accordance with <a href="https://www.osha.gov/ergonomics" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color:#2E7D32;font-weight:700;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:#4CAF50;text-underline-offset:3px;">OSHA ergonomic guidelines</a> for public facility safety standards.</p>
            <ul class="of-row__bullets">
              <li>
                <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
                ANSI Z97.1 and ASTM F1642 rated — meets public facility standards
              </li>
              <li>
                <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
                Holds glass intact on breakage — no flying fragments
              </li>
              <li>
                <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
                Invisible after installation — no visible change to station aesthetics
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Row 3: Solar Control & Glare — image LEFT, text RIGHT -->
        <div class="of-row">
          <div class="of-row__image">
            <img
              src="/images/mb-glare.jpg"
              alt="Airport terminal waiting area with comfortable passengers seated near large windows — soft filtered natural light, no harsh glare, mass transit window film"
              width="680" height="480" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
            />
          </div>
          <div class="of-row__text">
            <div class="of-row__eyebrow">Benefit 03 · Solar Control &amp; Glare</div>
            <h3 class="of-row__title">Passenger Comfort Across Every Waiting Area</h3>
            <p class="of-row__body">South- and west-facing terminal windows at PHL and the grand hall glazing at 30th Street Station create intense solar heat gain in passenger waiting areas. Seating near west-facing windows becomes uncomfortable during peak afternoon hours, and HVAC systems cycle continuously trying to compensate. High-VLT <a href="/benefits/glare-reduction/" style="color:#2E7D32;font-weight:700;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:#4CAF50;text-underline-offset:3px;">glare reduction film</a> cuts solar heat gain by up to 79% while maintaining the bright, open feel that modern transit architecture requires.</p>
            <p class="of-row__body">At SEPTA Regional Rail stations with glazed platform canopies — like Suburban Station and Temple University Station — solar film makes platform waiting comfortable year-round. Passengers stop avoiding sun-exposed seating. Staff workstations near perimeter glass maintain a consistent temperature without relying on portable cooling units.</p>
            <ul class="of-row__bullets">
              <li>
                <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
                Up to 79% solar heat reduction — passenger areas stay comfortable
              </li>
              <li>
                <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
                High-VLT film preserves the open, light-filled character of transit architecture
              </li>
              <li>
                <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
                Reduces hot spots near south and west-facing glazing without blackout film
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Row 4: Energy Savings — text LEFT, image RIGHT -->
        <div class="of-row of-row--rev">
          <div class="of-row__image">
            <img
              src="/images/mb-energy.jpg"
              alt="Large transit station or airport terminal exterior showing expansive glass facade — energy management and HVAC efficiency with airport window film in Philadelphia"
              width="680" height="480" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
            />
          </div>
          <div class="of-row__text">
            <div class="of-row__eyebrow">Benefit 04 · Energy Savings</div>
            <h3 class="of-row__title">Managing Energy Costs in Large Glass Structures</h3>
            <p class="of-row__body">Airports and transit stations are among the most energy-intensive commercial building types, according to the <a href="https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/use-of-energy/commercial-buildings.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color:#2E7D32;font-weight:700;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:#4CAF50;text-underline-offset:3px;">U.S. Energy Information Administration</a>. Their defining feature — expansive glass for natural light and wayfinding — is also their largest source of uncontrolled solar heat gain. Airport window film in Philadelphia terminals cuts solar heat transmission by up to 79%, directly reducing the HVAC load across gate areas, ticketing halls, and concourses.</p>
            <p class="of-row__body">For SEPTA and Amtrak facilities, Low-E insulating film adds a winter benefit — reflecting interior heat back into waiting areas through single-pane glazing common in older Philadelphia station buildings. Year-round, <a href="/benefits/energy-savings/" style="color:#2E7D32;font-weight:700;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:#4CAF50;text-underline-offset:3px;">energy savings</a> from reduced solar load and improved thermal performance contribute directly to lower utility costs across the facility portfolio.</p>
            <ul class="of-row__bullets">
              <li>
                <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
                Up to 79% reduction in solar heat gain through treated glazing
              </li>
              <li>
                <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
                Low-E film adds winter insulation for older single-pane station glass
              </li>
              <li>
                <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
                Works across the full facility — terminals, concourses, ticketing, offices
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

