          <div class="of-ftype__content">
            <div class="of-ftype__step">01</div>
            <h2 class="of-ftype__title">Solar Control Film.
Passenger Comfort at Scale.</h2>
            <p class="of-ftype__desc">The workhorse of airport and transit window film. Solar control film blocks incoming solar heat before it reaches passengers — reducing HVAC load, eliminating hot spots in gate areas and waiting halls, and cutting energy costs across the full facility portfolio. Available across 3M Prestige, LLumar, Vista, and Solar Gard in spectrally selective, neutral, and reflective finishes.</p>
            <div class="of-ftype__faq">
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Will solar film darken our terminal windows or change the open feel passengers expect?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>High-VLT solar control films like 3M Prestige 70 transmit 70% of visible light — the terminal stays bright and open. The film filters infrared heat (up to 79% blocked) and UV (99% blocked) without affecting the architectural character of the space. From outside, the glass has a subtle, nearly invisible tint.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Our gate windows face west and the afternoon sun makes the seating area unusable. What film works here?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Spectrally selective solar control film is specifically designed for this scenario. It targets the infrared portion of the solar spectrum — blocking heat — without significantly reducing visible light. Passengers near west-facing gate windows get comfortable seating; the view and bright atmosphere remain intact.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can film handle the large format glazing panels typical of modern airport terminals?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes — film is applied in panels and seamed to fit any glazing configuration, including floor-to-ceiling curtain wall panels, oversized fixed glazing, and angled glass. Our installers are experienced with the large-format commercial glazing common in PHL's terminal expansions.</div>
              </div>
            </div>
