          <div class="of-ftype__content">
            <div class="of-ftype__step">03</div>
            <h2 class="of-ftype__title">Safety &amp; Security Film.
Glass Stays. Danger Doesn't.</h2>
            <p class="of-ftype__desc">3M Scotchshield Ultra safety films hold glass fragments in place if breakage occurs — protecting passengers from flying shards regardless of the cause. Rated to ANSI Z97.1 and ASTM F1642, security film is the standard specification for public facility glass safety at airport terminals, SEPTA stations, and intercity rail hubs throughout Philadelphia.</p>
            <div class="of-ftype__faq">
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What security film standard applies to airport and transit facilities?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>3M Scotchshield Ultra safety films are rated to ANSI Z97.1 (safety glazing) and ASTM F1642 (blast resistance). For airport and transit applications, these are the key standards. The film holds glass fragments in place on breakage — whether from an accident, structural stress, or a security incident — protecting passengers from flying shards.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Does security film affect the appearance of our ticketing windows and security checkpoints?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Clear safety films are optically clear — staff and passengers see no difference after installation. For ticketing windows where some visual distinction is appropriate, we can combine safety film with a subtle tint or frosted lower section. The structural protection is invisible.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can safety film be layered with solar control film for gates that need both?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Dual-function films combine safety performance (ASTM F1642 rated) with solar control — single application that handles both heat rejection and fragment retention. This is a common specification for PHL gate windows facing runways, where both solar load and safety requirements apply.</div>
              </div>
