          <img src="/images/mb-slider-lowe.jpg" alt="Philadelphia train station exterior in winter — warm amber light from inside terminal through energy-efficient Low-E window film" class="of-ftype__bg" />
          <div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div>
          <div class="of-ftype__content">
            <div class="of-ftype__step">05</div>
            <h2 class="of-ftype__title">Low-E Climate Film.
Warm in Winter. Cool in Summer.</h2>
            <p class="of-ftype__desc">Low-emissivity (Low-E) insulating film delivers year-round thermal performance for older Philadelphia station buildings — reflecting interior heat back into waiting areas in winter and blocking solar heat gain in summer. The 3M Thinsulate CC75 is the benchmark specification for SEPTA and Amtrak facilities with original single-pane glazing.</p>
            <div class="of-ftype__faq">
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Our older SEPTA stations have single-pane glazing throughout. Can film improve winter performance?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes — Low-E insulating film (like 3M Thinsulate CC75) improves thermal performance of single-pane glass in both directions: it reflects interior heat back into the waiting area in winter and blocks solar heat gain in summer. For older Philadelphia station buildings with original single-pane glazing, Low-E film is often the most cost-effective upgrade available.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Does Low-E film affect radio frequency transmission? We have communications equipment near station glass.<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Standard Low-E films contain a thin metallic coating that can attenuate some RF signals. If this is a concern for your communications infrastructure, we specify non-metallic spectrally selective films — like 3M Prestige series — that achieve similar solar performance without affecting RF transmission. This is a common consideration for transit control facilities.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can Low-E and solar control film be applied to the same glass?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>3M Thinsulate CC75 is a dual-function film that delivers both solar control in summer and improved insulation in winter in a single application. It's designed for year-round climates like Philadelphia's — hot summers and cold winters — making it one installation that addresses both seasonal challenges.</div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <a href="/contact/" class="of-ftype__cta">Get a Low-E film estimate →</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SLIDE 06: Exterior Window Film -->
        <div class="of-ftype__slide" data-slide="5">
          <img src="/images/mb-slider-daylight.jpg" alt="Transit station concourse with exterior-mounted film on canopy glazing — bright diffused daylight, modern architecture" class="of-ftype__bg" />
          <div class="of-ftype__overlay"></div>
          <div class="of-ftype__content">
            <div class="of-ftype__step">06</div>
            <h2 class="of-ftype__title">Exterior Film.
Canopies. Skylights. Curtain Wall.</h2>
            <p class="of-ftype__desc"><a href="/benefits/exterior-window-film/" style="color:#a5d6a7;font-weight:700;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:#81c784;text-underline-offset:3px;">Exterior-mounted window film</a> is UV-stabilized, scratch-resistant, and engineered for outdoor exposure — station canopy glazing, skylight panels, and external curtain wall sections where interior installation isn't accessible. Installed from outside the station with no disruption to interior operations or passenger flow.</p>
            <div class="of-ftype__faq">
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Our station canopy glass is exposed to weather and cleaning equipment. Can film be applied externally?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Exterior-mounted window film is specifically engineered for outdoor exposure — UV-stabilized coating, scratch-resistant surface, and durability against weather cycling. It's used on station canopy glazing, skylight panels, and external curtain wall sections where interior installation isn't accessible.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">How long does exterior film last compared to interior film?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Quality exterior film has a manufacturer-warranted lifespan of 7–10 years in outdoor exposure, compared to 10–15 years for interior film. In transit environments with regular pressure washing, we specify the appropriate film grade for the cleaning protocol used by your maintenance team.</div>
              </div>
              <div class="of-ftype__faq-item">
                <button class="of-ftype__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can exterior film be applied while the station remains operational?<span class="of-ftype__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
                <div class="of-ftype__faq-a" hidden>Yes. Exterior application accesses glass from outside the station — no disruption to passengers or operations inside. Installation is scheduled around maintenance windows, track access hours, or off-peak periods to minimize any impact on transit operations.</div>
